air-column-answer-why =
    Air is matter, and the Earth pulls it as it pulls everything. Without gravity the gases would fly off into space; as it is, the air lies on the Earth in a layer, and each layer presses with its weight on the ones beneath. So the air's pressure near the ground is the weight of the whole column of air above each square metre: p = m·g / S. The higher you go, the less air is left above — and the lower the pressure; a town high in the mountains has less of it on the ground already. The air below is squeezed by the weight of the layers above, so it is denser. We do not feel these tonnes because the air presses on us equally from every side, and the gases and liquids of our body press back from within. And since air has weight, bodies in it feel a buoyant force — that is what holds an airship up: the helium in its envelope is lighter than the air it pushes aside. (Hence the joke that named the band Led Zeppelin: “it would go over like a lead balloon” — lead is far denser than air.)

    The pressure here is the standard atmosphere's — a day of "average" weather; on a real day the barometer shows a few hectopascals more or less, but the difference between heights hardly changes. The storey's height (2.8 m) is an estimate: the density worked out depends on it.
